 Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)*. 1316–1325.\n\n## <span id=\"page-14-18\"></span>A Convergence and Optimality Guarantees\n\nWe adopt the notation and constraints of § 6.1. We use  $\\mathcal{M}$  to index both modules and stages; the egress module is e. A scheduling strategy is  $\\mathbf{s} = \\{s_m\\}_{m \\in \\mathcal{M}}$ , where  $s_m = \\{(b_m^n, p_m^n)\\}_{n \\in \\mathcal{M}}$  denotes, for stage m, the batch assignments\n\n<span id=\"page-15-0\"></span> $b_m^n$  and SM percentages  $p_m^n$  for each module n. The effective batch of module n is  $b^n = \\sum_{m \\in \\mathcal{M}} b_m^n = k^n b^e$  with integer multipliers  $k^n \\geq 1$ . Stage latency is  $\\mathcal{L}(s_m) = \\max_{n \\in \\mathcal{M}} \\mathcal{L}_n(s_m)$  and end-to-end latency is  $\\mathcal{L}(s) = \\sum_{m \\in \\mathcal{M}} \\mathcal{L}(s_m)$ . The SLO requires  $\\mathcal{L}(s) \\leq \\Delta$ . Throughput is  $\\mathcal{T}(s) = b^e/\\mathcal{L}(s)$ .\n\n**Algorithmic move set. Algorithm 1** initializes from the best *coupled* (monolithic) feasible strategy and then iteratively: (i) picks a module n and proposes to increase its batch by one multiplier unit  $k^n$ ; (ii) tries placing this increment in each stage  $m \\in \\mathcal{M}$ ; (iii) rebalances the stage's SM percentages to minimize the induced increase in  $\\mathcal{L}(s_m)$  (\"Minimum Negative Increment\"); and (iv) commits the least-harmful feasible placement *only if* it does not reduce  $\\mathcal{T}(s)$ . The search stops when no such single-increment move exists.\n\nAssumptions. (A1) Finite discrete space. For each module n, the admissible batch  $b^n$  lies on a finite grid determined by SLO. Let B be the global maximum egress batch in § 6.1. By the coupling constraint  $b^n = k^n b^e$ , we set  $B^n = k^n B$  and restrict candidates to  $\\{0, k^n, 2k^n, \\ldots, B^n\\}$ . (A2) Feasibility oracle. For any candidate increment and within-stage SM reallocation, feasibility  $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbf{s}) \\leq \\Delta$  is testable via the profiled latency model. (A3) Monotone latency. Increasing a module's batch weakly increases its own latency and does not decrease others' latency within the same stage; increasing a module's SM share weakly decreases its latency while (under fixed 100% SM per stage) weakly increasing the rest.\n\n**Theorem A.1** (Feasibility invariants). Starting from the coupled feasible initialization, every accepted update preserves all constraints (per-stage SM budget, batch coupling, and SLO). Hence all intermediate and final strategies are feasible.\n\n*Proof.* Each candidate increment respects coupling by adding an integer multiple  $k^n$ ; the stage's SM percentages are rebalanced to satisfy the per-stage SM budget; the update is committed only if the recomputed latency obeys  $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbf{s}) \\leq \\Delta$ . Therefore, feasibility is an invariant by construction.  $\\square$ \n\n**Theorem A.2** (Finite-time convergence and complexity). *Algorithm 1* terminates after at most  $B - b^e_{\\text{init}} \\leq B$  accepted increments, where B is the global maximum egress batch (cf. Assumption A1) and  $b^e_{\\text{init}}$  is the egress batch in the coupled initialization. Moreover, the algorithm performs at most\n\n$$O(|\\mathcal{M}|^2 \\cdot (B - b_{\\text{init}}^e) \\cdot P)$$\n\nstage evaluations, where  $P \\leq 100$  upper-bounds the discrete SM reallocation trials per stage.\n\n*Proof.* By the coupling constraint  $b^n = k^n b^e$ , any accepted update that adds one multiplier unit  $k^n$  to some  $b^n$  is equivalent to increasing the egress batch by one. Assumption (A1) restricts  $b^e$  to  $\\{0,1,\\ldots,B\\}$ , hence at most  $B-b^e_{\\text{init}}$  accepted increments are possible, proving termination. For the complexity bound, in each accepted step the algorithm may examine  $O(|\\mathcal{M}|)$  choices of the module n,  $O(|\\mathcal{M}|)$  candidate\n\n<span id=\"page-15-2\"></span>![](_page_15_Figure_11.jpeg)\n\n**Figure 23.** Percentage of active SMs for BLIP3o-NEXT-3B with varying batch size in RTX 3090 GPU.\n\nstage placements m, and up to P SM reallocations, yielding  $O(|\\mathcal{M}|^2 \\cdot P)$  stage evaluations per accepted step. Multiplying by the number of accepted steps  $B - b_{\\text{init}}^e$  gives the bound.  $\\square$ \n\n**Theorem A.3** (Monotone improvement and 1-exchange optimality). Let  $\\{\\mathbf{s}_t\\}_{t\\geq 0}$  denote the subsequence of strategies produced by **Algorithm 1** immediately after each accepted update, with  $\\mathbf{s}_0$  being the coupled initialization, and let  $\\mathbf{s}^*$  be the final returned strategy. Then: (i) for every  $t\\geq 0$ ,  $\\mathcal{T}(\\mathbf{s}_{t+1})\\geq \\mathcal{T}(\\mathbf{s}_t)$ ; and (ii)  $\\mathbf{s}^*$  is 1-exchange optimal: there is no single feasible increment (increasing any  $b^n$  by  $k^n$  and rebalancing SMs within any one stage) that yields strictly higher throughput.\n\n*Proof.* (i) By design, the algorithm commits an update only if it does not reduce throughput, hence  $\\mathcal{T}(\\mathbf{s}_{t+1}) \\geq \\mathcal{T}(\\mathbf{s}_t)$  for all  $t \\geq 0$ . (ii) Upon termination, every single-increment proposal either violates feasibility ( $\\mathcal{L}(\\cdot) \\leq \\Delta$ ) or fails the non-decreasing-throughput acceptance test; therefore no strictly improving 1-exchange move exists, establishing 1-exchange optimality.\n\n### <span id=\"page-15-1\"></span>**B** Extending to Diffusion-based Modules\n\nIn § 3.1, we show that reusing unimodal serving systems for BLIP/BLIP-2–style multimodal models leads to pronounced GPU under-utilization. Here we extend the evaluation to a newer architecture BLIP3o-NEXT [2] whose output module is diffusion-based. It first uses an autoregressive (AR) module to produce intermediate features, and then conditions a diffusion decoder on these features to synthesize images.\n\nAs illustrated in Figure 23, we observe a clear asymmetry between the two phases of BLIP3o-NEXT: (i) the AR phase exhibits persistently low SM utilization and shows weak sensitivity to batch size; (ii) the diffusion phase is computedominated and its latency grows sharply with batch size. Our module multiplexing is expected to exploit this heterogeneity by overlapping a portion of diffusion denoising with the AR compute and rebalance per-stage SM shares to preserve the SLO. This scheduling will increase GPU utilization and shorten end-to-end latency. This experiment is intended to surface the headroom of module multiplexing in diffusion-augmented multimodal pipelines and to motivate dedicated diffusion-aware designs as future work."}}}}
